---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: null
description: A shared vocabulary for describing government, built as a profile of schema.org and established open standards.
---
<div class="gs-hero py-5">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="col-lg-8">
        <p class="text-uppercase small fw-semibold text-body-secondary mb-2">Version {{ site.version }}</p>
        <h1 class="display-5 mb-3">A shared vocabulary for describing government</h1>
        <p class="lead mb-4">{{ site.description }}</p>
        <p class="mb-4">
          <a href="/spec/" class="btn btn-primary">Read the specification</a>
          <a href="/profiles/core/" class="btn btn-outline-secondary ms-2">Browse the core profile</a>
        </p>
        <p class="small text-body-secondary mb-0">
          Namespace <code class="gs-ns">{{ site.namespace }}</code>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container py-5">
  <div class="row g-4 mb-5">
    <div class="col-md-4">
      <div class="card h-100"><div class="card-body">
        <h2 class="h5">A profile, not a new standard</h2>
        <p class="mb-0 small">schema.org covers about half of what a government publishes. Where it fits, gov-schema uses it directly. Where it has nothing, a mature open standard already exists, so gov-schema adopts that and defines a schema.org projection.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-md-4">
      <div class="card h-100"><div class="card-body">
        <h2 class="h5">A place is not an organization</h2>
        <p class="mb-0 small">A city is a territory; the city government is a body. Territories have no employees or budgets. They are separate entities linked by <code>areaServed</code>, and the validator enforces it.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="col-md-4">
      <div class="card h-100"><div class="card-body">
        <h2 class="h5">Works across countries</h2>
        <p class="mb-0 small">No national identifier system, government tier, or naming convention is built in. Identity is scheme-qualified, so an entity carries as many identifiers as it has.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="h4 mb-3">Domain coverage</h2>
  <div class="table-responsive mb-5">
    <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
      <thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>schema.org</th><th>Source standard</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for b in profiles.built %}
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{{ b.name }}</strong></td>
          <td class="small">{{ b.schemas | length }} schemas</td>
          <td>{{ b.source }}</td>
          <td><span class="badge text-bg-success">implemented</span></td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
        {% for p in profiles.planned %}
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{{ p.title }}</strong></td>
          <td class="text-body-secondary small">see profile</td>
          <td>{{ p.source }}</td>
          <td><span class="badge text-bg-secondary">planned</span></td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="h4 mb-3">The namespace</h2>
  <p class="small text-body-secondary">These are not documentation pages. Every published gov-schema document pins these exact URLs, and they are immutable once released.</p>
  <ul class="list-unstyled gs-mono small">
    <li class="mb-1"><a href="/v1/context.jsonld">/v1/context.jsonld</a> — the JSON-LD context</li>
    <li class="mb-1"><a href="/v1/govschema.ttl">/v1/govschema.ttl</a> — the RDF vocabulary</li>
    {% for b in profiles.built %}{% for s in b.schemas %}
    <li class="mb-1"><a href="{{ b.urlBase }}{{ s.file }}">{{ b.urlBase }}{{ s.file }}</a></li>
    {% endfor %}{% endfor %}
  </ul>
</div>
